\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>ma[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>] \u003Cbr id=\"al2-interpretive\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]ḍosin[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>] \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">·\u003C/span>TI&nbsp;&nbsp;MA[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cbr id=\"al2-diplomatic\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>].OSIN[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"facsimile-images\">\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>\u003Ca href=\"ISic004386_tiled.tif\">\u003Cimg title=\"Scan of Isler 2009: taf.16.9\" src=\"ISic004386_tiled-thumb.tif\">\u003C/a>\u003Ca href=\"ISic004386.jpg\">\u003Cimg title=\"Scan of Isler 2009: taf.16.9\" src=\"ISic004386-thumb.jpg\">\u003C/a>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"apparatus\">\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text from photograph of Isler 2009: taf.16.9\u003C/span>\u003C/span>; \u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"line 1\">line.1: \u003Cspan>Isler -]Ṭ(?)TIMA; the trace of an upper horizontal is visible, but this could be compatible with F or E also\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"translation\">\u003Ch2> translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">  \u003C/p>\u003Cp>  \u003C/p>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"commentary\">\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>As Isler observes, the nature of the blocks and the lettering mean that this must be part of a monumental text (from a building?), not dissimilar to those found in the theatre. The reading is not entirely clear, and the significance of ḌOSIN in a Latin text is very unclear (perhaps the end of a Greek name rendered in Latin?). On the basis of both architectural form and lettering, Isler reasonably suggests a late Republican date.\u003C/p>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"bibliography\">\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Isler 2009 101 with taf.16.9 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002631\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002631\u003C/a>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"handnote\">\u003Cp>Plainly carved monumental letters without serifs.\u003C/p> Line 1-2  100  Interlineation line 1 to 2    \u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"license\"> Licensed under a Creative Commons-Attribution 4.0 licence.  \u003C/div>",[186,189,192,195,198,201],{"id":187,"html":188},"editions","\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-interpretive\" lang=\"la\">  \u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">.\u003C/span>ti\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\">(vac.1)\u003C/span>ma[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>] \u003Cbr id=\"al2-interpretive\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]ḍosin[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>] \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Jonathan Prag\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"la\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">·\u003C/span>TI&nbsp;&nbsp;MA[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003Cbr id=\"al2-diplomatic\">\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">2\u003C/span>[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>].OSIN[\u003Cspan dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gap\">---\u003C/span>]\u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>",{"id":190,"html":191},"apparatus","\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text from photograph of Isler 2009: taf.16.9\u003C/span>\u003C/span>; \u003C/li>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan class=\"line 1\">line.1: \u003Cspan>Isler -]Ṭ(?)TIMA; the trace of an upper horizontal is visible, but this could be compatible with F or E also\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>",{"id":193,"html":194},"translation","\u003Ch2> translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">  \u003C/p>\u003Cp>  \u003C/p>",{"id":196,"html":197},"commentary","\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>As Isler observes, the nature of the blocks and the lettering mean that this must be part of a monumental text (from a building?), not dissimilar to those found in the theatre. The reading is not entirely clear, and the significance of ḌOSIN in a Latin text is very unclear (perhaps the end of a Greek name rendered in Latin?). On the basis of both architectural form and lettering, Isler reasonably suggests a late Republican date.\u003C/p>  ",{"id":199,"html":200},"bibliography","\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Isler 2009 101 with taf.16.9 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002631\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002631\u003C/a>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u0